{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation after plan review. Addressed: batch-check now also asks for each revision's root tree, so a 'missing' cap only counts as absent when the revision is readable; an unreadable revision raises and becomes Unavailable (new test test_unreadable_revision_is_a_git_failure_not_a_missing_cap). Not covered, and recorded as a known residual: a readable root tree with an unreadable .github subtree, or a gitlink at the cap path, now reads as a missing cap where per-revision ls-tree/cat-file raised; neither occurs in a healthy repository. Regression guard: test_history_walk_cat_file_starts_do_not_grow_with_revisions asserts the cat-file process count is 5 for both 3 and 12 revisions. Evidence: oracle test 606 -> 140 git starts; under the 0.5 s-per-start shim 1 passed in 78.83 s (call 75.01 s); dogfood module under the same shim on origin/main passes, slowest call 122.10 s, so no marker. tests/unit/metrics: 180 passed, 2 skipped."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795246141750574001,"uuid":"c7be095b-8bd8-46b7-a453-e8a663a667e4"}